{
  "$comment": "Canonical cross-engine operator contract for RuleMind. Every evaluation engine (TypeScript packages/rule-engine, Python apps/python-executor, Kotlin sdk-android/rulemind-core, Dart sdk-flutter) MUST satisfy every case below. Conformance tests in each language load this file and assert their compare() returns `expected`. Fixtures use native JSON types and set fieldType on ordering/equality cases so all engines agree without relying on value-string-parsing differences. Do not weaken a case to make an engine pass \u2014 fix the engine. CONTRACT: ordered operators (>=,<=,>,<,between) are NUMERIC-ONLY in every engine \u2014 a non-numeric operand (a label, or a date/ISO string) makes the condition false via numeric coercion (Python _coerce_number, Kotlin/Dart numericOrNull, Rust core, and the TS engine's numericOrNull). Dates are therefore never ordered via the numeric operators; date == / != compare the parsed instant. These non-numeric ordering cases ARE asserted below (previously they diverged in the TS engine, which used a lexical string compare / Date.parse ordering \u2014 now fixed).",
  "version": 1,
  "operators": [
    {
      "op": "==",
      "arity": 1
    },
    {
      "op": "!=",
      "arity": 1
    },
    {
      "op": ">",
      "arity": 1,
      "numeric": true
    },
    {
      "op": ">=",
      "arity": 1,
      "numeric": true
    },
    {
      "op": "<",
      "arity": 1,
      "numeric": true
    },
    {
      "op": "<=",
      "arity": 1,
      "numeric": true
    },
    {
      "op": "between",
      "arity": 2,
      "numeric": true,
      "needsValue2": true
    },
    {
      "op": "in",
      "arity": 1,
      "set": true
    },
    {
      "op": "not_in",
      "arity": 1,
      "set": true
    },
    {
      "op": "regex",
      "arity": 1
    },
    {
      "op": "exists",
      "arity": 0
    },
    {
      "op": "!exists",
      "arity": 0
    }
  ],
  "cases": [
